nd of course that could also be applied  to other application  applications as well so in this case uh  we use the deep learning model to help  us improve the axial resolution of a of  the nuclei in a developing developing  embryo so on the on the right side you  can kind of see that the  resolution of the nuclei is much better  and the result of this is much more  obvious when we  apply segmentation to it and so with the  deep with the deep learning model that  we've applied we can detect that the  cells are missing in the lower  left corner  of this embryo much more effectively and  we can also get cell shapes that are  more representative of the actual nuclei  in the embryo and we can apply this  model  to the um to a live data set that will  allow us to not just um  uh reconstruct the  uh the embryo the embryo but also track  the individual nuclei  and its development over time  now that we've covered some examples of  how deep learning works in real life  applications let's talk about the  operating principles for deep learning  so if you have attended our previous  webinar this would be a refresher for  you  in traditional analysis rules are  created by humans and the data is put  through a program and we will get the  answers out of it  in ai framework the concept is flipped  on the set we can provide the data and  the answer in the form of ground truth  and the framework will come up with the  rules that maps the input data to the  answer and when we get new data we can  apply the rules are generated from the  framework to produce the answers that we  were looking for  there are many types of deep learning  algorithm so today i would like to focus  on one specific type called a  convolutional neural network  it is commonly used in image  classification applications so for  example networks like unet and arcan  uses this framework to create deep  learning models i won't go into many  details about how each of these networks  behave but generally speaking in a  convolutional neural network the input  image is sampled into smaller blocks and  each block passes through multiple  convolution layers to generate feature  maps which are abstractions of the image  that corresponds to a certain shape or  features  and some features can be prominently  represented in the data and those will  have a greater weight and in the  learning process the weights given to  each feature block are iteratively  changed until the input matches with the  output so let's say i have  up here we have an example of a picture  of a robot when we apply when it goes  through a convolutional neural network  it is going to be sampled into multiple  different blocks and each of these  blocks could be a feature map that is  abstracted from the image so for example  it could be one of the features that  could be generated  could be the segmented appearance of the  robot's arms and so and  another one could be the flat surfaces  that represents the robot's face and  feet so these could be some examples of  feature maps and these feature maps  could also be processed further and  subdivided and generate additional uh  feature maps and so on and so forth for  multiple uh through multiple layers so  in each of these layers all of the  information  from  uh from each of these feature maps  connects the um the input directly to  the output  and that's how a convolutional neural  network uh works on on principle but how  does it work in real life so let me give  you a quick example  so  um i'm going to show you the training  process in avia but you have other tools  are available that you can train the  models just the same so you start by  specifying the training data and you'll  need two sets of data the raw image and  the corresponding ground truth  next we specify the network that you  want to use and you can tune the  hyperparameter to adjust how the system  learns then when you start the training  process  the computer will handle the rest and  give you a model output that you can use  on other data sets  so let's take a look at what happens  during the learning process in a  convolutional neural network the input  image so that would be what's on the  left side of this  network chart  it will pass through the network to come  up with a prediction output image which  is represented by the crystal ball on  the right  then when you look at the prediction it  will be compared against the ground  truth  and the difference between the two is  the uh prediction error  in red in the um in the bar chart on the  on the left here  and during this pro uh the learning  process the network will then try to  minimize the error and carry it forward  for future predictions and there are two  key concepts that drive this process  called gradient descent and back  propagation  so what is gradient descent so you can  imagine an error function is a landscape  with many hills and valleys and the  current error rate is a location  somewhere on this landscape  the goal is  for gradient descent is to reach the  valley or the local minimum from the  slope that you're standing on and when  you reach the bottom of the valley so  this should give you the optimal  parameter or weight that minimizes the  error between your prediction and the  ground truth data that you put in  and this process is done over many steps  and you can adjust the steps by  adjusting what's called the learn rate  which would um impact how quickly the  training process converges to a minimum  minima excuse me or if it ever does it  all  but keep that keep in mind that the  value that since we're talking about a  landscape with many hills and valley the  valley that you are in currently could  be one of many and there could be other  values that will give you an even lower  error rate and throughout this training  uh the training process  the gradient descent process is going to  be repeated multiple times and with each  iteration it is possible that you can  find a more optimal solution  and if we look at the graphic at the top  you can see the error chart can be  represented by the u-curve and the error  rate is the red bulb and the gradient  descent would try to  reach the bottom of this u-curve by um  you'll go iterating for multiple steps  and that's what great and that's  gradient designs  another key concept for learning in a  convolutional neural network setup is  back propagation this describes the  entire process for which the weights at  each layer is optimized and the way that  this works is  the model works backwards from the final  convolution layer from the output um  using gradient descent to find the  optimal weights for each element within  the layer then moving one uh step uh up  from one one layer up or one step  further away from the output towards the  input  and repeat the same process and it will  continue to repeat until you reach the  input layer  and  so as you can see on the on this image  here that we start off from the top we  start from the layer on the right hand  side and over and it will iterate  through the each one and over time  certain connections will be strengthened  another will be weakened  and so over multiple uh iterative  process  of  a back propagation so you will the  layers and connections are going to be  continuously strengthened or weakened uh  depending on the error output and what  you will get in the final pro uh product  is a model that encodes the different  weights and filters that transform the  input image to the desired output  now that we've covered the principle of  how  deep learning works let's talk about how  to gather data to train the model i've  put together a couple of best practices  to help you get started  there are many types of network that  have different training data  requirements today i want to focus on  gathering data for training a  convolutional neural network like a unit  or rcam for this type of network you  will need a paired image of raw data and  ground truths  the raw data is your standard imaging  protocol so it can be tuned to minimize  the damage to the specimen due to light  exposure however it is recommended that  you maintain a minimum signal to noise  ratio of 3 and above and anything below  that you could see more errors from  model output  the ground truth is the optimal imaging  state for your sample meaning that it  has low noise and background signal  along with  the best resolution you can get possible  you can run the convolution to get  better quality outcomes or other methods  to our computational clearing  to improve the quality of uh of your  ground shoot data set  the image should ideally should maximize  the dynamic range and be free of any  over saturation and undesirable  artifacts or debris  so on the left side of the slide i have  uh example of what a raw and what a the  ground tree dataset looks like on the  top on the top we have the raw image  which is a quite a bit noisier and you  cannot make out as much of the  details of the mitochondria whereas as  the bottom image you uh we have the  ground troop which it has much better uh  resolution of the uh for the  mitochondria you can actually see the  membrane that up surrounds each  mitochondria and it's much has  a good dynamic contrast between the  background and foreground  and  free up any noise or debris or over  saturation  and so when you have  pairs of data that you're training with  it is important for the data to be  aligned between the raw and the ground  truth data here is an example of a model  out at the bottom you have  an example of a model output that was  trained on a confocal and set image of  nuclear pore complexes  looking at the area  where the red arrow highlights you can  see that  on the left side we have a model output  that is trained on data sets that were  not aligned and the model was not able  to resolve these three individual  nuclear pore complexes um  as well uh that you can see on in the  red arrow 