essed with a  model that is trained for each channel  separately  and if we look at just the red channel  output  you can see the specialized model  resolution is much better than the  generic model output  and in more extreme cases the model may  try to correct the data in a way that  takes away from the fidelity of the data  which you can see in the on the left  side and the at the bottom  if you look at these two images both  actually came from the same nuclear pore  complex raw image but  i used a different model  on each of them on the left side the  model that i applied was trained on the  nuclear pore complexes so and this  allows um with this model the  data actually sharpens to point like  objects  um contrast that to the right where the  model that i plot that i applied was  trained on microtubules which are longer  filamentous and when the model sees  these point objects in the raw image it  tries to stretch them out like filaments  and you can see there's uh  some uh small con connectivity between  some of these points that should not  have existed that is not good  and um what are some examples of data  that you should train separate models so  for example you have uh if you're using  different imaging modalities or  acquisition settings like magnification  or exposure  objects that are of different shape  profiles so let's say you're both spots  and  filaments  or stains that targets different parts  of the same object  so um like say if you have a membrane  stain and a lumen stain um and what i'd  like to point out when i talk um between  the difference between this slide and  the previous slide is that in this case  we want to train um separate models when  uh  we want to train separate models  and not blend the data sets from  different sample classes together  so let's say if you have a raw data set  for a wide field image and a ground  truth data set for confocal you can use  you can still train the model to  transform the wide field images to  co